+
+## 3. Quality Priorities
+
+**Status:** `partially resolved`
+
+**Evidence:**
+- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (lines 240–257) — Seven-layer safety architecture,
+  demonstrating security as implicit priority #1.
+- `docs/SECURITY.md` — Detailed safety layers, Git rules, escalation policies.
+- `docs/adr/001-matrix-synapse-keycloak.md` — Explicit trade-off: choosing
+  Synapse despite higher RAM consumption (500–800 MB vs 50–250 MB for Dendrite)
+  for the sake of feature completeness, documentation, and OIDC maturity.
+- `docs/decisions/playwright/` — Each decision includes "Consequences" and
+  "Alternatives Considered" sections that reveal trade-off reasoning.
+- `docs/HARDWARE_REQUIREMENTS.md` — Resource monitoring data that implicitly
+  prioritises stability (recommends 16 GB RAM, documents swap exhaustion as a
+  blocker).
+
+**Gap:** No explicit, ranked list of quality attribute priorities (e.g.,
+"security > maintainability > performance > cost"). Trade-offs are explained
+per-decision but not traceable back to a system-level quality contract.
+
+**Blocks:** No — existing decisions show consistent implicit priorities.
+However, a future product-selection decision with conflicting SEO attributes
+(e.g., lower-cost vs higher-security) has no tie-breaking rule.
+
+**Next:** Add a brief quality-attribute profile to `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` or a
+new `docs/QUALITY.md` that ranks the top 3–5 quality priorities and documents
+the tie-breaking rule.
+
