+## 4. Resilience and Recovery
+
+**Status:** `resolved`
+
+**Evidence:**
+- `docs/SERVICES.md` (lines 83–107) — Restart procedures, crash recovery, after
+  plugin deployment, manual debug commands.
+- `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` (lines 228–253) — Migration from old system with state
+  backup and restore.
+- `docs/PI_SERVICES.md` (lines 188–258) — Daily backup system with multiple
+  scripts, rotation strategy, documented stale-sync warning for rechenknecht.
+- `docs/matrix/runbook.md` — Complete runbook for start, stop, health
+  verification, upgrade, rollback, restore, credential rotation, emergency
+  isolation, and external fallback.
+- `docs/matrix/security-and-retention.md` (lines 79–94) — Incident response,
+  backup and credential exposure procedures.
+- `docs/matrix/storage-and-retention.md` (lines 157–198) — Full restore-input
+  inventory (R1–R6) with restore order and backup artifact list.
+- `docs/releases/matrix-profile-v1.md` — Release contract with rollback
+  classification (config-only, application-compatible, database-migration,
+  restore-required).
+
+**Gap:** Resilience is thoroughly documented for the Matrix stack. Core Pi
+services (Paperless, Gitea, Calibre-Web) have backup scripts but no formal
+rollback/restore test evidence in the repository. The "stale backup sync to
+rechenknecht" issue is acknowledged but unresolved (PI_SERVICES.md line 248).
+
+**Blocks:** No — the Matrix stack recovery evidence is a model for other
+services to follow. The core services gap is operational risk but not an
+architecture blocker.
+
+**Next:** Extend the release-profile pattern from Matrix to at least the
+Paperless and Gitea services, including a documented restore test.
+
