+## 5. Threat and Access Model
+
+**Status:** `partially resolved`
+
+**Evidence:**
+- `docs/SECURITY.md` — Token management, 7-layer safety architecture, Git safety
+  rules, escalation policies, known gotchas.
+- `docs/matrix/identity-and-access.md` — Detailed identity model with
+  authentication methods, token lifecycle, revocation procedures, enforcement
+  controls, and the hard constraint "no service account is granted server-admin
+  scope."
+- `docs/matrix/security-and-retention.md` — Registration policy, TLS/network
+  boundary, resource/abuse limits, retention and privacy, logging/redaction
+  rules, backup/incident response, and a full Threat-To-Control Matrix (RISK-01
+  through RISK-07).
+- `docs/keycloak-service-sso.md` — Token-preservation contract for OIDC migration.
+
+**Gap:** The threat model is comprehensive for Matrix but partial for the wider
+system. There is no documented threat model for:
+- The NAS (YeraNAS) — old QTS OS, SSH with password auth, wide-open NFS exports
+  (though restricted to 2 hosts).
+- The Pi's Docker attack surface beyond Matrix.
+- Physical access threats to rechenknecht or YeraNAS.
+- Supply chain threats (pinned images help, but no audit trail for upstream
+  compromise of pinned artifacts).
+
+**Blocks:** No — the Matrix security model is a template. The NAS and Pi
+container threats are real but part of the existing design and not unique to
+product selection.
+
+**Next:** Extend the Threat-To-Control Matrix pattern from Matrix to the overall
+system, covering at least the NAS, Pi Docker hosts, and the inter-node network.
+
