+## 10. Validation Criteria
+
+**Status:** `partially resolved`
+
+**Evidence:**
+- `docs/matrix/poc-plan.md` (lines 178–349) — Complete G1–G4 pass/fail budgets
+  with expected values, budgets, and measurement commands. Operator blackbox
+  procedure with explicit pass/fail decision.
+- `docs/decisions/playwright/evidence-provenance.md` — Manifest validation
+  contract (schema, invariants, result precedence).
+- `docs/decisions/playwright/artifact-security.md` — Sanitisation gate, bundle
+  validation, negative coverage tests.
+- `docs/decisions/playwright/runner-bootstrap.md` — PW-I01 verification contract
+  with 7 categories of mandatory tests.
+- `docs/service-release-contract.md` — Schema-validated JSON handoff with
+  state machine (awaiting_operator_approval, blocked_inactive_services,
+  no_service_changes).
+- `docs/matrix/primary-channel-declaration.md` — 8 explicit preconditions
+  (checklist-style) before Matrix can become primary internal channel.
+- `docs/releases/matrix-profile-v1.md` — Release contract with backup/restore
+  evidence requirements and smoke-test contract.
+
+**Gap:** Validation criteria exist at the *component/project level* (Matrix PoC,
+Playwright pipeline, service releases) but not at the *architecture level*:
+- How do we know the overall architecture is "good enough" to proceed to
+  product selection?
+- What is the minimum set of documented decisions required before property-
+  specific design begins?
+- There is no architecture-level acceptance test, review gate, or sign-off
+  procedure.
+- The Matrix pattern (G1–G4 budgets) is excellent but confined to one subsystem.
+
+**Blocks:** Only indirectly — the matrix PoC criteria show the right pattern,
+and the release contracts show good component-level validation. But the absence
+of architecture-level validation means there is no formal way to declare
+"architecture is complete enough for product selection." This blocks the
+transition from architecture phase to product-selection phase.
+
+**Next:** Define a lightweight architecture validation framework that:
+1. Lists the minimum set of resolved decision areas (this document can serve
+   as a starting point).
+2. Defines a go/no-go gate for moving to product selection.
+3. Specifies how architecture validation results are recorded and reviewed.
+4. Borrows the G1–G4 pass/fail pattern from the Matrix PoC plan.
+
